    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec required validating an E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code. I found the normalization logic in `OwnerTelephone.toE164(...)`, which previously only enforced the generic E.164 shape (`+` followed by 8\u201315 digits).\n\nIn `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`:\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map (a `LinkedHashMap`, longest-prefix first) mapping country code \u2192 required national-digit count: `\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`.\n- After the existing E.164 shape check, the code strips the `+`, finds the matching country-code prefix (checking `61` before `1` so they don't collide), and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2014 which the existing `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler` turns into a **400** \u2014 when the national part is the wrong length. Unknown country codes remain permissive (bounded only by the 8\u201315 E.164 range).\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (which posts `+61 123` \u2192 only 3 national digits) now returns 400, and the existing valid AU mobile flow (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes.",
